  "documentsIntro": "Three short study guides written by Aletheia to accompany reading and catalog study.",
  "documentsSearchHint": "Search documents",
  "documentsNoResults": "No document matches the search.",
  "documentsAttribution": "Original Aletheia documents; they do not reproduce licensed third-party works.",
  "documentReadingTitle": "How to read the Bible",
  "documentReadingSubtitle": "A simple four-step method: observe, interpret, apply, and pray.",
  "documentReadingBody": "## Before reading\n\nThe Bible is a library of books written in different periods. Before looking for an immediate application, locate the passage: who is writing, to whom, and what problem or promise appears in the context?\n\n## Observe\n\nRead slowly and write down what the text says, not what you expect it to say. Notice repeated words, people, places, contrasts, and commands. Good observation prepares good interpretation.\n\n## Interpret\n\nAsk what the passage meant to its first readers and how it fits the biblical story. Compare translations and consult the commentary or dictionary when a word or custom is unclear.\n\n## Apply and pray\n\nApplication grows from the meaning of the text, not the other way around. End by turning what you have seen into prayer: give thanks, confess, ask for help, and commit to one concrete step.",
  "documentTimelineTitle": "Biblical timeline",
  "documentTimelineSubtitle": "Four stages to orient yourself in the history of the Old and New Testaments.",
  "documentTimelineBody": "## Patriarchs and exodus\n\nGenesis presents Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob; Exodus narrates the liberation from Egypt and the covenant at Sinai. These are stories of calling, promise, and the formation of a people.\n\n## Monarchy and prophets\n\nSamuel, Saul, and David inaugurate the monarchy. The prophets call the people to covenant faithfulness and announce judgment and hope.\n\n## Exile and return\n\nThe fall of Jerusalem and the Babylonian exile mark a deep crisis. Ezra and Nehemiah tell of reconstruction; the psalms and prophets preserve the memory of waiting.\n\n## Jesus and the church\n\nThe Gospels present Jesus as the fulfillment of the promises. Acts and the letters describe the community that proclaims his death and resurrection.",
  "documentGlossaryTitle": "Study glossary",
  "documentGlossarySubtitle": "Four terms that appear again and again in biblical reading.",
  "documentGlossaryBody": "## Covenant\n\nA bond established by God with his people, with promises and commitments. It is not a contract between equals: God takes the initiative and sustains the relationship.\n\n## Grace\n\nThe favor God gives without it being a deserved reward. In the New Testament it is the basis of salvation and the Christian life.\n\n## Justification\n\nGod's act of declaring the sinner who believes to be righteous on the basis of Christ's work. It is not prior moral improvement but a new relationship with God.\n\n## Messianism\n\nThe hope for a king or envoy of God who will restore his people. The Gospels read Jesus as the fulfillment of that hope, both present and future.",
